1. Current Methods Used in Jordan (Expanded Analysis)
Agriculture in Jordan relies heavily on traditional and informal approaches for diagnosing plant diseases. This methods are often slow, inconsistent, and lack scientific accuracy.
• Reliance on Personal Experience
Most farmers attempt to diagnose diseases based on past experience or visual clues. This frequently leads to:
•	Misdiagnosis, especially when symptoms resemble nutrient deficiencies or pest damage.
•	Delayed intervention until symptoms become severe and harder to treat.
• Consulting Neighboring Farmers
Farmers often depend on advice from neighbors or relatives. Although useful socially, this method:
•	Spreads misinformation quickly.
•	Lacks scientific verification.
•	Can lead to improper chemical treatments.
• Agricultural Engineers Through Cooperatives and Field Visits
Farmers who can afford it rely on:
•	Cooperatives and government extension services.
•	Private agricultural engineers for paid field visits.
Challenges:
•	Engineer-to-farmer ratio is low.
•	Visit scheduling delays allow disease to progress.
•	Remote areas often lack access to experts.
• WhatsApp Groups and Photo Sharing
Farmers send photos in groups and wait for advice. Limitations:
•	Poor-quality images reduce accuracy.
•	Engineers may hesitate to diagnose via chat.
•	Advice is inconsistent and sometimes contradictory.
• Random Pesticide Use & Late Intervention
Due to lack of accurate diagnosis:
•	Farmers often apply pesticides without identifying the disease.
•	This increases costs and causes long-term soil damage.
•	Diseases spread faster when not treated properly early.
Conclusion:
The current system is reactive rather than preventive, causing preventable yield losses and unnecessary spending.
 
