B) Impact of Reducing Infectious Disease Spread 
Many plant diseases in Jordan—especially fungal and bacterial—are highly infectious and can move quickly across fields if not detected early.
• Spread Rate Without Early Detection
•	Fungal diseases: 35–50% spread in 5–10 days
•	Bacterial diseases: 20–40% spread in 14 days
•	Viral diseases (insect transmitted): 10–30% per growth cycle
•	Greenhouse conditions can push spread up to 60%
• Spread Reduction With Early Detection (via AI)
Estimated reductions:
•	Fungal diseases: 45–65%
•	Bacterial diseases: 40–60%
•	Viral diseases: 20–40%
• Financial Savings Example (Per Dunum)
If 40% of plants get infected without early detection:
•	~250 plants × 2 JOD = 500 JOD loss
With early detection reducing spread to 20%:
•	~100 plants × 2 JOD = 200 JOD loss
Savings:
300 JOD per dunum per season
For a 5-dunum farm → 1,500 JOD saved
JUST from reduced infectious spread.
• National-Level Savings
If only 10% of Jordan’s farmers (10,500 farmers) use the app:
→ 1,500 JOD savings per farm
→ National savings ≈ 15.75 million JOD yearly
This is in addition to other economic benefits.
 
3. Key Areas to Consider in the Feasibility Study
A. Technical Considerations
• Dataset Relevance for Jordanian Crops
We must ensure:
•	The AI model is trained on crops common in Jordan (tomato, olives, grapes, cucumbers, citrus).
•	The dataset includes images of real-world field conditions.
• Online vs. Offline Functionality
Because many farms have weak connectivity:
•	AI inference should run offline.
•	Data syncs later when internet is available.
• Continuous Model Improvement
The app should support:
•	Retraining based on user images.
•	Increasing accuracy over time.
•	Localization for Jordan’s climate and diseases.
 
B. Market Considerations
• Jordan’s Agricultural Landscape
Jordan has:
•	105,000+ active farmers.
•	Agriculture contributing 5.5% to GDP.
•	High dependency on key crops such as tomatoes, olives, grapes, dates, citrus, cucumbers.
• Technology Adoption & Smartphone Penetration
Smartphone usage:
•	National: 88%+
•	Rural areas: 70–78%
This makes mobile-based solutions very feasible.
• High-Priority Crops and Disease Types
The majority of disease cases in Jordan come from:
•	Early blight & late blight (tomatoes).
•	Olive peacock spot.
•	Powdery mildew (grapes, cucumbers).
•	Citrus canker.
These represent 60%+ of plant disease reports.
• Institutional Market Demand
Potential stakeholders:
•	Ministry of Agriculture
•	Jordan Farmers Union
•	NGOs & donor projects (FAO, ICARDA, Mercy Corps)
•	Large greenhouse operators
•	Export farms
 
C. Economic Considerations
• Development & Maintenance Costs
Approximate investment:
•	AI model development: $18,000–$30,000
•	Mobile app development: included above
•	Server & cloud hosting: $4,000–$8,000 yearly
•	Model updates & data labeling: $1,500–$3,000 yearly
• Economic Benefits to Farmers
Savings come from:
•	Reduced pesticide misuse.
•	Less reliance on experts.
•	Higher yield due to better disease control.
• Revenue Model Options
•	Freemium model (basic free, premium paid).
•	Monthly subscription for cooperatives.
•	National-level licensing.
•	Partnerships with seed & pesticide companies.
 
D. Operational Considerations
• Ease of Use in the Field
App must:
•	Work in sunlight, dust, humidity.
•	Handle low-quality images.
•	Guide users to take clear photos.
• Arabic Interface for Jordanian Farmers
•	Simple terminology.
•	Jordanian dialect support if needed.
•	Optional voice instructions.
 
E. Legal & Ethical Considerations
• Privacy & Data Protection
•	Secure storage of plant images.
•	Private and confidential handling of location data.
•	Compliance with local data laws.
• Accuracy Responsibility
•	Clear disclaimers that app guidance is advisory.
•	Recommendations should not violate pesticide regulations.
• Safe Agricultural Advice
•	Avoid dangerous chemical combinations.
•	Avoid recommending banned pesticides.
 
